    "q-137059": {
      "dom_id": "step-q137059",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{3}=1\\,\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{15}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $4\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(4\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{22}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{22}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{22\\times 1}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{22}{15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{22}{15}=1\\,\\text{R}7=1\\,\\frac{7}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
